From: Fabiano Tassotti <fabianotassotti@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: realtek: wait out the full hardware reset time after GPIO deassert

`714-01-net-dsa-rtl8365mb-wait-out-full-chip-reset.patch` already
established, from Realtek's own documentation, that this chip family
needs close to a full second to complete its internal bring-up after a
reset - that patch fixed the *software* reset path
(`rtl8365mb_reset_chip()`, `RTL8365MB_CHIP_RESET_REG`).

`rtl83xx_probe()` performs a separate, earlier *hardware* reset via the
optional `reset`/`reset-gpios` line, and only waits
`REALTEK_HW_START_DELAY` (100 ms) after deasserting it before returning
- an order of magnitude short of the same chip's documented bring-up
time. On a board whose switch is reset by this GPIO path (as opposed to
relying solely on the register-based reset), the very first MDIO access
in `priv->ops->detect()` can land before the chip's internal state
machine is actually ready, intermittently.

Measured on a Mercusys MR80X v5 (RTL8367S-VB, GPIO-reset via
`reset-gpios`): after a rapid sequence of driver unbind/rebind and
warm-reboot cycles, a subsequent boot occasionally came up with the
switch seemingly detected (chip ID read succeeds) but the CPU-facing
conduit never receiving anything from it - unbinding and rebinding the
MDIO device (which re-runs this same GPIO reset sequence, this time
with an operator-inserted multi-second gap before rebind) reliably
recovered it. That gap accidentally standing in for a missing hardware
wait is a symptom of the real bug: `REALTEK_HW_START_DELAY` is too
short, not that the driver needs a manual unbind/rebind workflow.

Raise it to 1000 ms, matching the bring-up time this same driver
already treats as authoritative for the register-based reset path in
`714-01`. `REALTEK_HW_STOP_DELAY` (the reset-asserted hold time) is
unrelated to bring-up and is left unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Fabiano Tassotti <fabianotassotti@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/realtek.h
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/realtek.h
@@ -14,7 +14,14 @@
 #include <linux/reset.h>
 
 #define REALTEK_HW_STOP_DELAY		25	/* msecs */
-#define REALTEK_HW_START_DELAY		100	/* msecs */
+/* Same bring-up time 714-01-net-dsa-rtl8365mb-wait-out-full-chip-reset.patch
+ * already established as authoritative for the register-based reset path,
+ * from Realtek's own documentation: the chip needs close to a full second
+ * to complete its internal bring-up after any reset, hardware or software.
+ * 100 ms here was intermittently too short and could leave detect() racing
+ * the chip's own internal state machine.
+ */
+#define REALTEK_HW_START_DELAY		1000	/* msecs */
 
 struct phylink_mac_ops;
 struct realtek_ops;
